-- #1002 regression fixture — TWO INTERFACES, ONE METHOD NAME.
--
-- Read test/references_fixtures/iface_collide/expected.golden as the assertion:
-- `alpha|method|tag` must hold Alpha's declaration plus the head inside
-- `impl Alpha P` and NOTHING ELSE; `beta|method|tag` likewise for `impl Beta Q`.
-- No head may appear under the other interface's key. (The golden carries the
-- exact line:col pairs — they are captured, not hand-counted here.)
--
-- This pins the F1 regression found reviewing the first cut of #1002. That cut
-- keyed an impl head by looking the bare METHOD name up in `useEnv`, which holds
-- exactly one slot per `method<TAB><name>` and carries NO interface identity — so
-- both heads landed on `alpha|method|tag` and `beta|method|tag` kept only its
-- declaration. That is strictly worse than the omission it replaced: `references`
-- on `Alpha.tag` returned Beta's impl head, and a rename would have EDITED it,
-- orphaning Beta's own method — the very failure mode #1002 exists to close, one
-- dimension over. The key is now derived from the interface NAME the impl header
-- spells (`ifaceModId`, compiler/tools/refindex.mdk).
--
-- No method is CALLED here, deliberately: two interfaces declaring `tag` are both
-- in dispatch scope, so any `tag` call would be genuinely ambiguous ("No impl of
-- Beta for P"). The collision belongs next to the impls, not next to a call.
-- `tag` IS imported from alpha here, deliberately: that is what puts a value in
-- `useEnv`'s single `method<TAB>tag` slot and makes the old method-name keying
-- attribute Beta's head to ALPHA — the confidently-wrong attribution, rather than
-- the merged-onto-`?ext` form. The complementary no-method-import spelling is
-- pinned by references_fixtures/impl_method/noimport.mdk.
import alpha.{Alpha, tag}
import beta.{Beta}

data P =
  | P

data Q =
  | Q

impl Alpha P where
  tag P = 1

impl Beta Q where
  tag Q = 2
